Good start, Boss 👊 — you’re on the right track with **LeetCode 1480 (Running Sum of 1d Array)**.  
But your code has a few issues:

---

### Problems in your code
1. Missing `;` after `int j = nums[0]`.  
2. Inside the loop, `j = ans[]` is incomplete (that’s why it won’t compile).  
3. Your logic can be simplified — you don’t actually need to store `nums[0]` separately if you iterate properly.

---

### 🔑 Running Sum Logic
For each index `i`, the running sum is:
```
ans[i] = ans[i-1] + nums[i]
```
with base case `ans[0] = nums[0]`.

---

### Fixed Version (C++)
```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> runningSum(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> ans;
        int sum = 0;   // keeps track of cumulative sum
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            sum += nums[i];     // add current number to cumulative sum
            ans.push_back(sum); // store running sum
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
```

---

### 🔥 Even Cleaner (In-place, O(1) extra space)
Instead of creating a new array, modify `nums` directly:
```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> runningSum(vector<int>& nums) {
        for (int i = 1; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            nums[i] += nums[i-1];
        }
        return nums;
    }
};
```

---

👉 So after your `int j = nums[0];`, you should loop and **keep adding nums[i] to j** and push that into `ans`.  
But the **cleaner in-place version** above is the one interviewers prefer.

---

